WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.475
- You wore bralettes in Korea?

00:00:01.475 --> 00:00:02.535
- Yeah, 'cause it's hot!

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.724
Ain't nobody going to Korea in the summer.

00:00:04.724 --> 00:00:06.564
You gon' die out there, are you kidding me?

00:00:06.564 --> 00:00:08.296
You need a bralette, I'm not gonna walk out there

00:00:08.296 --> 00:00:10.875
with no T-shirt on in the summertime in Korea, you crazy.

00:00:10.875 --> 00:00:11.708
- Your accent's coming out.

00:00:11.708 --> 00:00:12.683
- Yeah, your accent's coming out.

00:00:12.683 --> 00:00:13.516
(all laugh)

00:00:13.516 --> 00:00:17.433
(lighthearted xylophone music)

00:00:17.834 --> 00:00:19.823
- I'm here with my Korean-American friends

00:00:19.823 --> 00:00:22.823
to talk about growing up in America.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:26.133
^- I was born in Louisiana, grew up in Georgia,

00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:27.581
^I came here when I was 16.

00:00:27.581 --> 00:00:29.569
^- So I came to America, 2010,

00:00:29.569 --> 00:00:32.484
^after I graduated high school from Korea.

00:00:32.484 --> 00:00:34.426
^I used to live in Orange County area,

00:00:34.426 --> 00:00:35.702
^and moved to Fullerton

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:38.400
^and moved to LA about three years ago.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.671
^- I was born in San Francisco,

00:00:40.671 --> 00:00:43.968
^and I lived out in Korea since I was one,

00:00:43.968 --> 00:00:46.215
^and then I moved back here in second grade.

00:00:46.215 --> 00:00:47.302
^- I was born in Korea

00:00:47.302 --> 00:00:49.449
^but I moved to America when I was 12.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.058
^Since then I haven't been back yet.

00:00:51.058 --> 00:00:54.225
(upbeat string music)

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:55.510
- I went to the dentist,

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.436
I guess they couldn't remember my name,

00:00:57.436 --> 00:00:59.244
^and then they wrote on there (speaks foreign language)

00:00:59.244 --> 00:01:00.077
^(all gasp)
- Really?

00:01:00.077 --> 00:01:01.580
They just wrote (speaks foreign language) because they knew

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.214
I was speaking English, and I was just like,

00:01:03.214 --> 00:01:05.150
"Yo I'm Korean", like you know?

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.073
So I mean, that's gonna happen.

00:01:07.073 --> 00:01:08.859
- But these days, there is a lot of people who go

00:01:08.859 --> 00:01:11.030
out to Korea to teach English and stuff.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:11.863
- Yeah.

00:01:11.863 --> 00:01:13.048
- So you'll meet a lot of people

00:01:13.048 --> 00:01:15.028
who speak English just as much as you do.

00:01:15.028 --> 00:01:16.631
- There are a lot of foreigners living in Korea.

00:01:16.631 --> 00:01:19.497
- So I speak Korean with my parents, fully,

00:01:19.497 --> 00:01:22.037
but like because I did the growing up like here,

00:01:22.037 --> 00:01:24.783
I feel weird speaking Korean to people like my age.

00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:25.898
- Do you have an accent?

00:01:25.898 --> 00:01:26.731
- No, I don't.

00:01:26.731 --> 00:01:27.674
- I've never seen you talking.

00:01:27.674 --> 00:01:29.921
- My Korean language is like elementary school level,

00:01:29.921 --> 00:01:31.864
so I wouldn't know any of the new slang.

00:01:31.864 --> 00:01:33.023
I wouldn't know what to say.

00:01:33.023 --> 00:01:35.286
- I think when you go to Korea, the way

00:01:35.286 --> 00:01:37.226
everyone grows up is different, so

00:01:37.226 --> 00:01:38.059
- Yeah.

00:01:38.059 --> 00:01:39.805
- Even though I was raised in Korea like

00:01:39.805 --> 00:01:42.218
when I was young, and then I went back when I got older,

00:01:42.218 --> 00:01:44.994
I still had a depression because it was so different.

00:01:44.994 --> 00:01:47.577
(upbeat music)

00:01:48.031 --> 00:01:49.501
I go both ways.
- Yeah I go both ways.

00:01:49.501 --> 00:01:51.359
- But I still kinda root for Korea.

00:01:51.359 --> 00:01:54.526
- I think I would root for Korea more.

00:01:54.663 --> 00:01:59.251
- Korea versus America, I would for sure root for Korea,

00:01:59.251 --> 00:02:03.167
but America versus other countries, then of course America.

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:04.413
- Yes I think that way too, yes, yes.

00:02:04.413 --> 00:02:05.246
- Well said.

00:02:05.246 --> 00:02:07.047
I think that's what I was trying to say.

00:02:07.047 --> 00:02:09.402
(quick piano music)

00:02:09.402 --> 00:02:11.632
- So I watch more American TV shows,

00:02:11.632 --> 00:02:13.774
but I laugh more at Korean shows,

00:02:13.774 --> 00:02:15.515
it's just more relatable, it's funnier.

00:02:15.515 --> 00:02:17.530
They way they react with each other on TV,

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.339
I feel like Korea has a really good reality program,

00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:20.172
- Right.

00:02:20.172 --> 00:02:21.005
- It's like so funny.

00:02:21.005 --> 00:02:22.565
^I love (speaks foreign language)

00:02:22.565 --> 00:02:23.398
(all gasp)

00:02:23.398 --> 00:02:26.296
- Oh my gosh, The Animal, that's like my favorite TV show.

00:02:26.296 --> 00:02:27.760
^- (speaks foreign language) For sure.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.427
- Oh yeah, that one's funny too.

00:02:33.087 --> 00:02:35.025
- Sometimes when I watch American shows,

00:02:35.025 --> 00:02:36.938
I don't understand anything.

00:02:36.938 --> 00:02:37.957
- Me too, I don't get their humor,

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:40.076
like the dry humor is not funny to me.

00:02:40.076 --> 00:02:41.338
- I don't get the Korean.

00:02:41.338 --> 00:02:42.171
- Really?

00:02:42.171 --> 00:02:43.335
- Yeah, I have to look at the subtitles.

00:02:43.335 --> 00:02:44.168
You know the captions when they come up?

00:02:44.168 --> 00:02:45.001
- Yeah.

00:02:45.001 --> 00:02:45.912
- I'm like "Oh, that's what they said".

00:02:45.912 --> 00:02:48.298
- Korean reality shows are more like,

00:02:48.298 --> 00:02:51.045
people suffering, like in a funny way, you know?

00:02:51.045 --> 00:02:51.878
- Games.

00:02:51.926 --> 00:02:54.969
- Yeah games, and like they living in small houses,

00:02:54.969 --> 00:02:56.985
and like doing things challenges together,

00:02:56.985 --> 00:02:58.575
and I think it's funnier to see people like

00:02:58.575 --> 00:03:00.341
going through hard things,

00:03:00.341 --> 00:03:02.614
but like, laughing through it.

00:03:02.614 --> 00:03:06.120
- I find that like Korean variety shows it's more involved.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.537
Only celebrities.

00:03:07.576 --> 00:03:08.826
But in America,

00:03:09.363 --> 00:03:12.724
there are some regular people involved in the

00:03:12.724 --> 00:03:16.273
a lot of variety shows so I think that's, like that's cool--

00:03:16.273 --> 00:03:17.106
- Reality.
- Yeah.

00:03:17.106 --> 00:03:20.456
(upbeat xylophone music)

00:03:20.456 --> 00:03:21.722
- I grew up in (speaks foreign language)

00:03:21.722 --> 00:03:23.391
and they have a bunch of like Korean people.

00:03:23.391 --> 00:03:26.231
I definitely hung out with mostly all Koreans.

00:03:26.231 --> 00:03:29.160
- So when I came here, I didn't know who to hang out with

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.683
because Harris County just, is black and white and then me.

00:03:32.683 --> 00:03:34.078
You know, and my aunt, you know.

00:03:34.078 --> 00:03:35.289
But she don't go to school with me, you know.

00:03:35.289 --> 00:03:36.122
(all laugh)

00:03:36.122 --> 00:03:38.038
It was just like "Whoa!" So overwhelming for me

00:03:38.038 --> 00:03:40.733
so I had a really thick accent when I first came here,

00:03:40.733 --> 00:03:42.187
and they're like "You have an accent".

00:03:42.187 --> 00:03:43.999
I'm like, "No, you have an accent".

00:03:43.999 --> 00:03:44.874
(all laugh)

00:03:44.874 --> 00:03:47.474
And then I just felt kind of like,

00:03:47.474 --> 00:03:48.307
kind of like stuck.

00:03:48.307 --> 00:03:49.903
- One thing that really bothers me.

00:03:49.903 --> 00:03:52.804
If you act like too Asian, if you have Asian friends,

00:03:52.804 --> 00:03:54.541
if you look Asian, if you dress Asian,

00:03:54.541 --> 00:03:55.818
^you're called FOB, right?

00:03:55.818 --> 00:03:56.651
^- Right.

00:03:56.651 --> 00:03:57.699
- And people look down on you,

00:03:57.699 --> 00:03:59.244
people are like "why are you even in America"?

00:03:59.244 --> 00:04:01.980
If you act American, or if you have more white friends,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.308
or if you dress, let's say, not that Asian,

00:04:04.308 --> 00:04:05.645
you're called whitewashed.

00:04:05.645 --> 00:04:06.478
- Oh yeah.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.351
- Ever since I dyed my hair blonde,

00:04:08.351 --> 00:04:09.595
I've seen comments saying like,

00:04:09.595 --> 00:04:11.859
"Oh she's whitewashed, she's trying to be white".

00:04:11.859 --> 00:04:15.216
And maybe I dye my hair because I like the color,

00:04:15.216 --> 00:04:16.401
maybe I want to have a change,

00:04:16.401 --> 00:04:18.615
just like how everyone dyes their hair color.

00:04:18.615 --> 00:04:20.925
Growing up, especially in middle school,

00:04:20.925 --> 00:04:22.133
because of that FOB stereotype,

00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:23.701
I think that's why I tried so hard

00:04:23.701 --> 00:04:25.298
to hang out with American people.

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:27.238
And I wish I made more Korean friends there,

00:04:27.238 --> 00:04:30.130
but I was so worried that I would be called a FOB,

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:32.535
and then now like, people calling you whitewashed

00:04:32.535 --> 00:04:34.925
just because your acting not Asian,

00:04:34.925 --> 00:04:37.686
it's like so not fair to be on each side.

00:04:37.686 --> 00:04:39.593
I think we need to stop thinking

00:04:39.593 --> 00:04:40.426
we have to be one way or another.

00:04:40.426 --> 00:04:42.376
- But I think it's getting better than before.

00:04:42.376 --> 00:04:43.209
- Yeah, for sure.

00:04:43.209 --> 00:04:45.406
- Like these days I kind of feel that way.

00:04:45.406 --> 00:04:46.239
- Yeah.

00:04:46.239 --> 00:04:48.017
- I wish I came to American earlier,

00:04:48.017 --> 00:04:50.782
because I came to America when I was getting into college.

00:04:50.782 --> 00:04:53.342
So I didn't really get to hang out

00:04:53.342 --> 00:04:55.308
with white or black or like hispanic

00:04:55.308 --> 00:04:56.154
- Other races.

00:04:56.154 --> 00:04:57.229
- Yeah other races.

00:04:57.229 --> 00:05:00.895
I hung out with mostly Korean and other Asians

00:05:00.895 --> 00:05:02.397
like Taiwanese, Chinese.

00:05:02.397 --> 00:05:05.346
Whenever I meet other race friends in class,

00:05:05.346 --> 00:05:07.959
they would find Korean-related thing

00:05:07.959 --> 00:05:10.265
and they would only talk about like that.

00:05:10.265 --> 00:05:11.465
- Oh, like K-pop and stuff?

00:05:11.465 --> 00:05:13.995
- Yeah K-pop, or like "I've been to Korea",

00:05:13.995 --> 00:05:15.640
like my friend is Korean teacher.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.956
- You're good considering when you came by,

00:05:17.956 --> 00:05:19.028
you came in high school?

00:05:19.028 --> 00:05:19.861
- Yeah.

00:05:19.861 --> 00:05:21.016
- You're really, really good.

00:05:21.016 --> 00:05:21.849
- When I first met her,

00:05:21.849 --> 00:05:22.728
that's like one of the first things I said.

00:05:22.728 --> 00:05:25.698
I had no idea you came when you were 19.

00:05:25.698 --> 00:05:26.531
- That's really young.

00:05:26.531 --> 00:05:27.619
- That's crazy.

00:05:27.619 --> 00:05:28.869
- You're lying!

00:05:29.019 --> 00:05:31.602
(upbeat music)

00:05:32.223 --> 00:05:33.056
- English.

00:05:33.081 --> 00:05:33.914
- English.

00:05:33.914 --> 00:05:35.861
- When I get really mad, just English.

00:05:35.861 --> 00:05:37.703
- I don't cuss in Korea at all.

00:05:37.703 --> 00:05:40.522
When I cuss in English, it doesn't sound bad.

00:05:40.522 --> 00:05:42.212
- You know everybody says like (bleeps).

00:05:42.212 --> 00:05:44.099
In Korean if you're like (bleeps)

00:05:44.099 --> 00:05:45.464
(all laugh)

00:05:45.464 --> 00:05:47.246
You know? It actually sounds worse.

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:49.228
(upbeat music)

00:05:49.228 --> 00:05:50.061
- Tipping.

00:05:50.746 --> 00:05:51.579
- Oh tipping.

00:05:51.579 --> 00:05:53.026
Cause you don't tip in Korea.

00:05:53.026 --> 00:05:54.176
- No, not at all.

00:05:54.176 --> 00:05:55.458
- Now that I think about it,

00:05:55.458 --> 00:05:57.950
cause in Korea you don't tip and that sounds so nice.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:00.915
- But now I think in Korea they don't get tips,

00:06:00.915 --> 00:06:02.095
so I feel bad for them.

00:06:02.095 --> 00:06:03.739
- I literally taught my friend that's just

00:06:03.739 --> 00:06:05.956
just visiting from Korea how to tip, I was like

00:06:05.956 --> 00:06:06.902
(all exclaim)

00:06:06.902 --> 00:06:09.961
you just times this by 15% or 20%.

00:06:09.961 --> 00:06:10.794
- Yeah, yeah.

00:06:10.794 --> 00:06:12.109
- Another culture shock.

00:06:12.109 --> 00:06:15.101
This is a good thing but whenever like strangers

00:06:15.101 --> 00:06:16.018
they smile.

00:06:16.188 --> 00:06:17.378
- Yeah, when you walk by?

00:06:17.378 --> 00:06:18.211
- Yeah!

00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:19.225
- Like welcoming.

00:06:19.225 --> 00:06:20.058
- Very welcoming.

00:06:20.058 --> 00:06:21.884
- They don't like to talk to strangers in Korea,

00:06:21.884 --> 00:06:24.264
but here they're like "Hi"! Or whatever.

00:06:24.264 --> 00:06:25.906
- If you say hi to a stranger in Korea,

00:06:25.906 --> 00:06:28.318
they're like "Whoa, do I know you"?

00:06:28.318 --> 00:06:29.341
(all laugh)

00:06:29.341 --> 00:06:31.374
- You know your age, you're one year younger here.

00:06:31.374 --> 00:06:32.207
- Right.

00:06:32.207 --> 00:06:33.040
- And it just sucks,

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:34.460
cause sometimes I go to Korea and they're like,

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:38.256
"How old are you"? I'm like "Oh, I'm, example, 24".

00:06:38.256 --> 00:06:39.089
(all laugh)

00:06:39.089 --> 00:06:41.547
Right? And then like I come here and I'm like,

00:06:41.547 --> 00:06:44.230
"Oh I'm 24, oh just kidding I'm 22 or 23".

00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:46.542
(upbeat guitar music)

00:06:46.542 --> 00:06:48.719
- All of a sudden we're cool, but we were always cool.

00:06:48.719 --> 00:06:49.552
- Yeah.

00:06:49.552 --> 00:06:50.418
- You know what I mean?

00:06:50.418 --> 00:06:51.251
- Yeah!

00:06:51.251 --> 00:06:53.999
Without the K-pop, we're still us, like we're still cool.

00:06:53.999 --> 00:06:55.798
We can still dance, we can still sing, like.

00:06:55.798 --> 00:06:59.677
- But then again, that's what's making us more noticeable.

00:06:59.677 --> 00:07:02.736
K-pop, K-dramas, definitely put like a big, big,

00:07:02.736 --> 00:07:04.069
it does its job.

00:07:04.118 --> 00:07:05.073
- But we were always cool.

00:07:05.073 --> 00:07:06.486
- We were always cool.

00:07:06.486 --> 00:07:07.395
- Everybody's just more aware.

00:07:07.395 --> 00:07:08.802
- I'm really just thankful, though

00:07:08.802 --> 00:07:09.695
that it's getting known here.

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:10.528
- Oh no, of course.

00:07:10.528 --> 00:07:13.111
^(upbeat music)

00:07:15.635 --> 00:07:19.823
- Like you have to be brave and be ready to embrace.

00:07:19.823 --> 00:07:21.730
- I think like when you first come,

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:24.539
you need to kind of feel out of your circle.

00:07:24.539 --> 00:07:25.777
Even if you're uncomfortable,

00:07:25.777 --> 00:07:27.728
try your best to learn English.

00:07:27.728 --> 00:07:29.811
So adapt to this culture,

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:31.862
and then you can still keep your culture

00:07:31.862 --> 00:07:33.690
where you came from, which is Korea,

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:35.960
that way you can really become like a Korean-American.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:37.266
- Try to keep both cultures.

00:07:37.266 --> 00:07:39.216
You don't want to grow up only speaking English

00:07:39.216 --> 00:07:40.688
and forgetting the Korean side.

00:07:40.688 --> 00:07:41.688
You want to keep the both sides.

00:07:41.688 --> 00:07:44.005
- I think both cultures have amazing things.

00:07:44.005 --> 00:07:45.874
- And I was a super shy kid when I was little,

00:07:45.874 --> 00:07:47.518
I know it's really hard to tell now.

00:07:47.518 --> 00:07:48.351
(all laugh)

00:07:48.351 --> 00:07:49.601
- Hard to tell.

00:07:49.963 --> 00:07:51.575
- There's a lot of mean kids out there,

00:07:51.575 --> 00:07:52.991
when those kids would pick on me,

00:07:52.991 --> 00:07:56.193
I would go home crying like "Am I ugly"?

00:07:56.193 --> 00:07:57.501
Like "What's wrong with me"?

00:07:57.501 --> 00:07:58.848
Like "Is there something wrong with me"?

00:07:58.848 --> 00:08:00.917
I didn't understand what was going on, but

00:08:00.917 --> 00:08:01.806
you know that's just ignorance.

00:08:01.806 --> 00:08:02.639
Be fearless.

00:08:02.639 --> 00:08:03.472
- Yeah, be fearless.

00:08:03.472 --> 00:08:04.305
- Do whatever you want to do.

00:08:04.305 --> 00:08:05.138
- Be you, yeah.

00:08:05.138 --> 00:08:05.971
- Yeah.

00:08:05.971 --> 00:08:09.471
(lighthearted jazz music)

